[38;5;231m⏺[39m Good — that means designing for arbitrary field sets. Next fork, and it's the one that decides how much this thing is:

  [1mThe fork:[0m right now [38;5;153mapp.js[39m does three separate jobs in the submit handler — reads values out of the DOM, validates
  them, and reports the error to the console. "Reusable" can mean extracting just the middle job, or all three. This is
  not cheap to change later: it sets whether every future form writes its own submit handler or just declares rules.

  - [1mPure validation only[0m — a module that takes a plain data object plus rules and returns errors. Buys you the smallest,
    most testable surface and works anywhere (Node tests too). Costs: each new form still hand-writes DOM reading and
    error display, so that boilerplate gets duplicated instead.
  - [1mValidation + form binding[0m — the module also attaches to a [38;5;153m<form>[39m, reads its fields, and renders errors next to
    inputs. Buys you genuinely one-line new forms. Costs: more code, now couples to DOM structure and needs a convention
    for where error text goes; harder to unit test without a DOM.
  - [1mPure core + thin optional binder[0m — two layers, the binder built on the core. Buys both, and the core stays testable.
    Costs: more moving parts than a one-form app strictly needs today.

  [1mMy recommendation: pure core + thin optional binder.[0m With "more forms coming, shapes unknown," the duplicated
  DOM-and-error-display boilerplate is the part that will actually hurt across five forms, and layering it keeps the
  validation logic itself dependency-free and unit-testable. But it is the most code, so I want your call.
